time-services for Debian
========================

System user and RTC ownership
----------------------------

The daemon runs as the system user and group "_qcom-time", created during
package configuration from /usr/lib/sysusers.d/time-services.conf. The
executable is /usr/sbin/time_daemon.

A udev rule gives /dev/rtc0 ownership root:_qcom-time and mode 0640. The
daemon only reads the RTC using RTC_RD_TIME; it adjusts the system clock
using settimeofday(2). The service grants CAP_SYS_TIME for these updates
and restricts device access to reading /dev/rtc0.

The rule matches add and change events. When udev is running, package
configuration creates the account, reloads the rules and triggers a change
event for rtc0, waiting for it to finish before starting the service.
An installation into an offline filesystem applies the rule at boot.
Administrators can override the packaged rule through /etc/udev/rules.d.

State
-----

Time offsets are persisted in /var/lib/time, created by the service unit's
StateDirectory= and owned by _qcom-time. The path is compiled into the
daemon (OFFSET_LOCATION in time_genoff.h) and is not configurable.
UMask=0077 makes newly created offset files readable and writable only by
the service user. Existing files retain their permissions.

Hardware requirements
---------------------

time_daemon talks to the modem over QMI and is useful on Qualcomm platforms
with a modem exposing the QMI time service through libqmi-framework. Both
the daemon and its service configuration select /dev/rtc0 explicitly.

The daemon changes the system clock, so the package conflicts with other
providers of time-daemon, including chrony and systemd-timesyncd.
